Implement Gitea issue #775: Add administrator frontend for API consumer and key management.

You are working in the Sport Analytics Tool / Stat'sTheGame repository.

IMPORTANT PROJECT RULES

1. Read and follow the repository instructions before changing anything, including:
   - AGENTS.md / AGENTS.override.md if present
   - docs/git-methodology.md
   - docs/ai/usage.md
   - relevant frontend/design/testing documentation
   - docs/api/consumer-keys.md
   - docs/api/openapi.md
   - docs/api/overview.md
   - relevant security/authentication documentation
   - the current issue #775
   - user-feedback issue #612 where relevant

2. Follow the approved GitHub Flow adapted for Gitea.
   - Do not work directly on main.
   - Do not commit or push directly to main.
   - The GitHub repository is only a mirror. Do NOT push changes or create a PR against the GitHub mirror.
   - Work against the actual project repository/Gitea workflow.
   - If you are already on an appropriate issue branch, use it.
   - Otherwise create:
     feat/775-api-consumer-admin-ui

3. Multiple developers are working in this repository.
   - Do not rewrite, remove or reformat unrelated work.
   - Keep the diff scoped to this issue.
   - Reuse existing components, API clients, patterns and styles rather than introducing parallel abstractions.
   - Do not modify unrelated backend behaviour merely to make the frontend easier to implement.

4. Comply with the project's AI policy.
   - Update the repository AI usage/declaration records required by the current policy.
   - Any generated code committed as part of this work must use the repository-required AI attribution.
   - Use the correct model declaration for this work: Codex[GPT-5.6 Sol], unless the actual Codex model/runtime identifies itself differently, in which case record the actual model.
   - Do not fabricate manual work, test results or evidence.

GOAL

Add a first-class administrator UI for managing external API consumers using the backend capabilities that already exist.

Administrators should not need Swagger/API Explorer, PowerShell or another HTTP client for routine API consumer/key management.

This UI should complement, not replace, the API documentation/API Explorer.

FIRST: INVESTIGATE THE CURRENT IMPLEMENTATION

Before editing code:

1. Inspect the current frontend:
   - apps/frontend/src/features/admin/
   - AdministrationPage
   - admin API client conventions
   - authentication/authorization hooks
   - routing
   - shared form/dialog/status components
   - responsive styling
   - existing frontend tests
   - Playwright admin journeys

2. Inspect the current backend/contracts/OpenAPI implementation for API consumers:
   - list API consumers
   - create API consumer
   - rotate key
   - revoke/disable functionality
   - consumer details
   - per-consumer usage
   - consumer-self usage
   - quotas/rate limits

3. Inspect docs/api/consumer-keys.md and the live shared contracts/OpenAPI definition.

4. Determine whether administrator access to a selected consumer's usage ACTUALLY exists.

There is a known possible discrepancy:
- #612 describes an administrator/reviewer finding a particular consumer's usage.
- Existing documentation may expose only GET /api/v1/consumer/usage for the calling API key's own usage.

Do not assume either state.

If admin per-consumer usage is not supported:
- do not query PostgreSQL/Supabase directly from the frontend;
- do not weaken authorization;
- do not invent an undocumented endpoint as incidental scope;
- implement the rest of this issue;
- document the gap clearly;
- prepare the text for a small follow-up Gitea backend issue;
- report this to me at the end.

Before making changes, give me a short implementation plan based on the actual repository state, then proceed unless a genuine blocker requires my decision.

REQUIRED UI

Add API consumer management to the existing Administration area.

Expected information architecture:

Administration
├── Users & access
├── API consumers
│   ├── Consumer list
│   ├── Create consumer
│   └── Consumer detail
│       ├── Configuration
│       ├── Key management
│       └── Usage, where the backend supports admin access
└── Data governance

Follow existing application visual conventions instead of creating a new design system.

ADMINISTRATION ENTRY POINT

Add a clear "API consumers" section/action to the existing Administration page.

It must:
- appear only in the existing administrator experience;
- explain briefly what it manages;
- lead to the API-consumer management page;
- preserve existing Users & access and Data governance functionality.

CONSUMER LIST

Provide a useful responsive list/table/card representation using data actually returned by the backend.

Display safe metadata such as:
- consumer name;
- consumer ID;
- active/revoked state if available;
- per-minute rate limit;
- daily quota;
- created/updated information where useful.

Never display:
- raw API keys except the one-time issue/rotation response;
- stored hashes;
- bearer tokens;
- secrets.

Support:
- loading;
- success;
- empty;
- forbidden;
- API error/retry states.

CREATE CONSUMER

Provide an accessible form using fields required by the existing backend contract.

Likely fields include:
- name;
- rate limit per minute;
- daily quota;

but inspect the contract and use the authoritative field names/constraints.

Requirements:
- clear labels and units;
- client-side validation that mirrors, but does not replace, backend validation;
- useful backend validation messages;
- successful creation refreshes/updates the relevant consumer state;
- the raw key returned from creation is presented in a dedicated one-time secret state.

ONE-TIME KEY PRESENTATION

This is security-sensitive.

When the backend returns a raw API key following creation or rotation:

- clearly state that this is the only time the complete key will be shown;
- provide an accessible "Copy API key" control;
- provide clear success/failure feedback for copying;
- do not put the key in the URL;
- do not persist it to localStorage/sessionStorage;
- do not log it;
- do not include it in analytics;
- do not refetch it;
- discard it from UI state when the user leaves/dismisses the one-time-key screen;
- ensure normal consumer-list/detail views never display it afterwards.

Do not weaken the backend's one-time secret semantics.

KEY ROTATION

Where supported by the current API:
- expose Rotate key;
- require an explicit confirmation;
- explain that the previous active key will stop working;
- call the existing backend endpoint;
- show the new raw key only in the one-time secret view described above;
- refresh safe key/consumer metadata afterwards.

REVOKE / DISABLE

Where supported by the current API:
- expose the appropriate action;
- use the terminology used by the backend;
- require a clear destructive confirmation;
- provide success/error feedback;
- refresh the consumer state.

Do not add fake revoke behaviour if the backend does not support it.

CONSUMER DETAIL

Provide a focused detail view for a selected consumer.

Show:
- safe identity/configuration;
- quota/rate-limit configuration;
- key state/metadata that the backend safely exposes;
- management actions;
- usage information if admin per-consumer usage is supported;
- a link to the API documentation/API Explorer.

Do not duplicate the entire OpenAPI documentation inside this page.

USAGE

If administrator per-consumer usage is implemented by the backend, expose it here.

Make the following understandable using only data actually supported by the contract:
- consumer identity;
- requested/default time window;
- UTC dates/time interpretation;
- route/operation;
- request count;
- response status class;
- quota relationship where meaningful.

Include loading, empty and error states.

Never expose:
- raw key;
- key hash;
- Authorization/X-API-Key header values;
- request bodies;
- unrelated sensitive information.

If admin usage is NOT implemented, do not fake this section. Document and report the missing backend capability.

API DOCUMENTATION RELATIONSHIP

Add an obvious route/link from API consumer administration to the existing API docs/API Explorer.

Do not move these user-testing goals into the admin UI:
- PUB-05 API discovery
- API-02 aggregate API understanding
- API-03 deprecation/migration understanding

Those should continue to be testable through the API product/documentation.

This frontend primarily improves API consumer administration and the API-04 workflow for #612.

ACCESSIBILITY

Follow existing project accessibility standards.

At minimum:
- full keyboard operation;
- semantic headings/forms/tables;
- associated form labels;
- programmatic validation errors;
- visible focus;
- no colour-only status meaning;
- accessible loading/error/status announcements;
- accessible confirmation dialogs;
- dialog initial focus/focus containment/focus restoration consistent with existing components;
- copy-success feedback accessible to screen readers.

RESPONSIVE DESIGN

Verify the management workflow at the same representative desktop/mobile sizes used elsewhere in the project.

A wide desktop table may become cards or a reduced-column representation on mobile if that matches existing project patterns.

Do not introduce horizontal overflow for the main workflow.

TESTS

Add focused automated tests for the implemented behaviour.

At minimum cover:
- Administration page contains API Consumers entry point for admins;
- access control;
- consumer list success;
- consumer list empty state;
- consumer list API error/retry;
- create form;
- client/backend validation;
- successful creation;
- one-time raw-key display;
- copy-key interaction;
- key not displayed after one-time view is dismissed;
- rotation confirmation/success/error where supported;
- revoke/disable confirmation/success/error where supported;
- usage loading/success/empty/error where supported;
- secret values are not accidentally rendered in ordinary list/detail state.

Add/extend Playwright coverage for the critical admin journey at representative desktop and mobile widths.

Use mocked/test credentials only. Never place a real API key in test fixtures.

DOCUMENTATION

Update only documentation affected by the implementation.

At minimum inspect:
- docs/api/consumer-keys.md
- docs/api/overview.md
- relevant administration/user-facing documentation
- any feature/status/index docs that would otherwise become inaccurate
- relevant AI usage register/declaration required by project policy

Do not describe planned functionality as implemented.

If admin usage is unavailable, explicitly document the current boundary and produce a proposed follow-up issue rather than claiming it exists.

VERIFICATION

Run the project's normal checks relevant to the changed scope.

At minimum attempt:
- formatting / Prettier
- frontend lint
- frontend typecheck
- focused frontend tests
- affected repository tests
- production frontend build
- relevant Playwright tests
- repository-wide check/ci command required by current project docs where practical

If a check cannot run because of environment/tooling rather than the code:
- report the exact command;
- report the exact blocker;
- do not claim it passed.

Do not "fix" unrelated pre-existing failures as part of this issue unless they directly block verification. Report them separately.

GIT / COMPLETION

Do not merge the branch.
Do not push to the GitHub mirror.
Do not commit directly to main.

When implementation and verification are complete, show me:

1. repository state you found;
2. implementation summary;
3. changed files;
4. backend contracts/endpoints reused;
5. whether administrator per-consumer usage exists;
6. security handling of one-time API keys;
7. tests added/changed;
8. every command run and its result;
9. documentation updated;
10. any limitations/follow-up issue needed;
11. git diff --stat;
12. git status;
13. suggested Conventional Commit message;
14. suggested PR title and complete PR description.

The commit message must follow the repository methodology and AI policy. Suggested form:

feat(admin): add API consumer management

Refs #775
Assisted-by: Codex[ACTUAL_MODEL]

Do not create a commit or PR until I review the completed implementation unless the current task explicitly authorizes you to do so.

