content.text:
Implement Gitea issue #776: Add administrator per-consumer API usage visibility.

You are working in the Sport Analytics Tool / Stat'sTheGame repository.

This is a follow-up to #775 and is required for the API-04 workflow in Sprint 3 user-feedback issue #612.

==================================================
PROJECT / REPOSITORY RULES
==================================================

Before changing anything:

1. Read and follow all repository instructions, including:
   - AGENTS.md
   - AGENTS.override.md if present
   - docs/git-methodology.md
   - docs/ai/usage.md
   - docs/api/consumer-keys.md
   - docs/api/openapi.md
   - docs/api/overview.md
   - relevant security/authentication documentation
   - relevant backend/frontend testing documentation
   - relevant user-testing documentation for #612 / API-04
   - the current implementation associated with #775

2. Follow the project's GitHub Flow adapted for Gitea.
   - Do NOT work directly on main.
   - Do NOT push directly to main.
   - Do NOT push to or create a PR against the GitHub repository; it is only a mirror.
   - Work against the actual project/Gitea repository.
   - Multiple developers are working on this repository, so do not alter unrelated work.

3. Comply with the project AI policy.
   - Update the required AI usage/declaration records.
   - Do not invent test evidence.
   - Use the actual Codex model/runtime in the AI attribution.
   - If it is Codex GPT-5.6 Sol, use:
     Assisted-by: Codex[GPT-5.6 Sol]

==================================================
DEPENDENCY ON #775
==================================================

#776 builds on #775, which added the administrator API-consumer frontend.

FIRST inspect the Git state and determine whether #775 is:

A. already merged into main;
B. present on the current branch but not merged;
C. available on another local/remote feature branch;
D. not available locally.

Do not duplicate #775's work.

If #775 is already in main:
- update from the latest main;
- create:
  feat/776-admin-consumer-usage

If #775 is not yet merged but the current worktree contains its completed implementation:
- preserve that work;
- create a stacked branch for #776 from the #775 branch only if doing so will not overwrite or mix unrelated work;
- clearly report that #776 is stacked on #775 and that #775 must merge first.

If the correct base cannot be determined safely:
STOP and tell me exactly what state you found instead of guessing.

Do not modify or reset unrelated developer changes.

==================================================
ISSUE GOAL
==================================================

Add administrator-authorized per-consumer API usage visibility.

Currently:

GET /api/v1/consumer/usage

is authenticated using an API consumer key and exposes usage only for that calling consumer.

An administrator currently cannot inspect the usage of a selected API consumer without possessing that consumer's secret key.

Implement a safe administrator workflow that allows an authenticated application administrator to inspect aggregated API usage for a selected API consumer.

The administrator must NEVER need the consumer's raw API key.

This issue includes:

1. backend administrator usage endpoint;
2. shared contracts;
3. OpenAPI;
4. backend/database/service coverage;
5. frontend integration into the API Consumers UI from #775;
6. documentation;
7. API-04 readiness for #612.

==================================================
FIRST: INVESTIGATE THE EXISTING IMPLEMENTATION
==================================================

Before writing code, inspect the implementation of:

- GET /api/v1/consumer/usage
- consumer API-key authentication
- usage telemetry collection
- usage repository/service layer
- usage aggregation/grouping
- retention/window rules
- rate limits and quotas
- API consumer repository/service
- administrator authorization middleware
- existing admin API-consumer routes/controllers
- shared Zod schemas/types
- OpenAPI definitions
- #775 frontend API consumer components/API client
- frontend authentication/admin access patterns
- backend/API/database tests for consumer usage
- frontend/Playwright tests added by #775

Identify which logic can be reused.

Do NOT implement a second independent usage calculation path if the existing consumer-self usage service/repository can be safely generalized or reused.

Before editing code, give me a concise implementation plan based on what actually exists, then proceed unless a genuine architectural/security decision requires clarification.

==================================================
BACKEND API
==================================================

Add an administrator-authorized handwritten API operation for usage of a selected API consumer.

A likely route is:

GET /api/v1/admin/api-consumers/{consumerId}/usage

However:

- inspect existing route naming and architecture first;
- use the repository's established convention;
- do not force this route if the existing API structure provides a more consistent shape.

Requirements:

- authentication is application-user bearer authentication;
- authorization requires administrator role;
- consumer identity is selected by safe server-side consumer ID;
- no API key is supplied to this endpoint;
- usage is scoped exclusively to the selected consumer.

Reuse the same semantics as GET /api/v1/consumer/usage where appropriate.

That includes, where currently implemented:

- bounded UTC date window;
- default lookback;
- maximum date range;
- pagination/result limit;
- normalized route/operation grouping;
- HTTP status class grouping;
- request count;
- retention boundaries.

Do not create subtly different usage definitions between consumer-self and administrator views unless there is a documented reason.

==================================================
AUTHORIZATION / VISIBILITY
==================================================

Inspect how administrator ownership/visibility of API consumers currently works.

The existing implementation may have an administrator-owned consumer model.

Do not assume all administrators can inspect all consumers unless that is already the project's authorization model.

Use the existing API-consumer visibility/ownership rules.

Required behavior:

- unauthenticated request -> 401;
- authenticated non-admin -> 403;
- administrator who is allowed to see the selected consumer -> allowed;
- invalid or non-visible consumer -> safe response consistent with the project's non-disclosure conventions.

Do not leak whether an inaccessible consumer exists.

==================================================
SECURITY / PRIVACY
==================================================

The administrator usage response must NEVER contain:

- raw API keys;
- API-key hashes;
- Authorization header values;
- X-API-Key values;
- bearer tokens;
- request headers containing credentials;
- request bodies;
- response payloads;
- raw query-string values;
- unrelated personal information.

The administrator workflow must not:

- require retaining a consumer API key;
- recover a previously issued key;
- query Supabase/PostgreSQL directly from the frontend;
- bypass backend authorization;
- weaken the consumer-self security model.

Keep GET /api/v1/consumer/usage working as consumer-self access.

Do not introduce a breaking change to that route.

==================================================
RESPONSE CONTRACT
==================================================

Prefer reusing the existing usage response model where possible.

The administrator response should provide only the information needed to understand safe aggregate usage, for example:

- selected consumer identity:
  - consumer ID;
  - consumer name where safe/useful;
- requested/default usage window;
- UTC date/time interpretation;
- normalized operation/route;
- HTTP status class;
- request count;
- quota/rate-limit context where available and meaningful.

Do not add fields merely because they are present internally.

If the self-usage response can be reused unchanged plus safe consumer context, prefer that over creating unnecessary parallel schemas.

==================================================
SHARED CONTRACTS
==================================================

Add/update shared Zod schemas and TypeScript types for the administrator usage endpoint.

Requirements:

- validate path parameters;
- validate query parameters;
- validate response payloads;
- preserve the same range/limit constraints as consumer-self usage where applicable;
- avoid duplicated constants/rules where a shared definition can safely be reused.

Do not introduce frontend-only interface definitions that drift from the shared API contract.

==================================================
OPENAPI
==================================================

Add the administrator usage operation to the authoritative OpenAPI specification.

Document:

- administrator bearer authentication;
- consumer ID path parameter;
- supported date/window parameters;
- limit/pagination behavior if applicable;
- successful response;
- 400 validation failures;
- 401 authentication failure;
- 403 authorization failure;
- safe not-found/non-visible behavior according to repository conventions;
- UTC/grouping semantics;
- security/privacy behavior at an appropriate level.

Run OpenAPI lint and contract tests.

Do not alter unrelated API operations.

==================================================
BACKEND IMPLEMENTATION
==================================================

Follow existing architecture.

Prefer a flow such as:

route
 -> authentication/authorization
 -> controller
 -> service
 -> existing usage repository/query logic

rather than placing SQL/business logic directly in the controller.

Reuse the current usage aggregation implementation wherever possible.

If refactoring existing self-usage code is necessary to support both:

- keep the refactor small;
- preserve existing behavior;
- add regression tests for consumer-self usage;
- avoid unrelated cleanup.

==================================================
FRONTEND — #775 API CONSUMER UI
==================================================

Extend the administrator API Consumers UI from #775.

On the selected consumer detail route:

/admin/api-consumers/:consumerId

add a clear Usage section.

The administrator should be able to answer:

- which operations/routes the consumer has used;
- when the usage occurred;
- how many requests were made;
- which response status classes occurred;
- what time window is being viewed;
- how this relates to quota/support investigation where sufficient data exists.

Do not require the administrator to know or enter an API key.

==================================================
FRONTEND USAGE STATES
==================================================

Implement:

- loading state;
- populated usage state;
- valid empty-usage state;
- validation/error state;
- authorization state where appropriate;
- retry behavior.

Clearly distinguish:

"No usage exists in this period"

from:

"Usage could not be loaded."

Do not treat an empty result as an error.

==================================================
USAGE PRESENTATION
==================================================

Use existing visual/frontend conventions.

Show only fields actually supported by the contract.

A table/card presentation may include:

- date / UTC period;
- operation or normalized route;
- HTTP status class;
- request count.

Where quota context exists safely, expose it in a way that is understandable without implying unsupported real-time precision.

Do not display internal IDs where they add no value to the administrator.

Do not show:
- raw keys;
- key hashes;
- request URLs containing user query values;
- credential material.

==================================================
TIME WINDOW
==================================================

If the existing consumer usage API supports configurable date ranges:

- expose the same capability in the admin UI;
